# Typescript Testing Rules

This document defines the testing standards and practices for Typescript.

## Core Requirements

### Test Framework

- Use NodeJS native test runner for all testing
- Use `describe` and `it` for test structure
- Use `chai` expect assertions for assertions
- Place all test files in the `test` folder

### Running Tests

- **`yarn test`**: Run all tests if they exist
- **`yarn test:unit`**: Run unit tests if they exist
- **`yarn test:integration`**: Run integration tests if they exist
- **`yarn test:e2e`**: Run end-to-end tests if they exist
- **Important**: The `yarn test:*` commands do not accept extra parameters
- **For specific tests**: Use `tsx` with proper command line arguments for the NodeJS native test runner

**Examples:**

```bash
# Run all tests
yarn test

# Run only unit tests
yarn test:unit

# Run only integration tests
yarn test:integration

# Run only e2e tests
yarn test:e2e

# Run a specific test file
tsx --test test/unit/services/user-service.test.ts

# Run tests with specific pattern (NodeJS native test runner syntax)
tsx --test --test-name-pattern="should validate email" test/unit/

# Run tests with coverage
tsx --test --test-reporter=spec --test-reporter=lcov --experimental-test-coverage test/unit/

# Run tests with custom reporter
tsx --test --test-reporter=spec test/unit/
```

## Best Practices

### Test Structure

```typescript
import { describe, it } from 'node:test';
import { expect } from 'chai';

describe('ComponentName', () => {
  describe('methodName', () => {
    it('should do something specific', () => {
      // Arrange
      const input = 'test';

      // Act
      const result = method(input);

      // Assert
      expect(result).to.equal('expected');
    });
  });
});
```

### HTTP Mocking

- Use `nock` to mock HTTP responses for unit tests
- Mock external API calls to ensure test isolation
- Verify that mocked endpoints are called with expected parameters

```typescript
import nock from 'nock';

describe('API Client', () => {
  it('should fetch user data', async () => {
    // Arrange
    const mockUser = { id: 1, name: 'Test User' };
    nock('https://api.example.com').get('/users/1').reply(200, mockUser);

    // Act
    const result = await apiClient.getUser(1);

    // Assert
    expect(result).to.deep.equal(mockUser);
  });
});
```

### Test Data Generation

- Use `fishery.Factory` for generating test data
- Create factories for complex objects to ensure consistent test data
- Use descriptive factory names that indicate the purpose

```typescript
import { Factory } from 'fishery';

const UserFactory = Factory.define<User>(() => ({
  id: 1,
  name: 'Test User',
  email: 'test@example.com',
  createdAt: new Date(),
}));

describe('UserService', () => {
  it('should create a new user', () => {
    // Arrange
    const userData = UserFactory.build();

    // Act
    const result = userService.createUser(userData);

    // Assert
    expect(result).to.have.property('id');
    expect(result.name).to.equal(userData.name);
  });
});
```

### TDD Workflow Examples

#### Red Phase - Writing Failing Test

```typescript
describe('Calculator', () => {
  it('should add two numbers', () => {
    // This test will fail until we implement the add method
    const calculator = new Calculator();
    const result = calculator.add(2, 3);
    expect(result).to.equal(5);
  });
});
```

#### Green Phase - Minimal Implementation

```typescript
class Calculator {
  add(a: number, b: number): number {
    return a + b; // Minimal implementation to make test pass
  }
}
```

#### Refactor Phase - Improve Code Quality

```typescript
class Calculator {
  add(a: number, b: number): number {
    if (typeof a !== 'number' || typeof b !== 'number') {
      throw new Error('Both arguments must be numbers');
    }
    return a + b;
  }
}
```

### Testing Considerations

#### Unit Tests

- Test individual functions and methods in isolation
- Mock dependencies to ensure test isolation
- Focus on testing the specific behavior of the unit under test
- Use meaningful test descriptions that explain the expected behavior

```typescript
describe('UserValidator', () => {
  it('should return true for valid email', () => {
    const validator = new UserValidator();
    const result = validator.isValidEmail('test@example.com');
    expect(result).to.be.true;
  });

  it('should return false for invalid email', () => {
    const validator = new UserValidator();
    const result = validator.isValidEmail('invalid-email');
    expect(result).to.be.false;
  });
});
```

#### Integration Tests

- Test the interaction between multiple components
- Use real dependencies when possible
- Test complete workflows and user journeys
- Verify that components work together correctly

```typescript
describe('UserService Integration', () => {
  it('should create user and send welcome email', async () => {
    // Arrange
    const userData = UserFactory.build();
    const emailService = new EmailService();

    // Act
    const user = await userService.createUser(userData);
    const emailSent = await emailService.sendWelcomeEmail(user);

    // Assert
    expect(user).to.have.property('id');
    expect(emailSent).to.be.true;
  });
});
```

#### E2E Tests

- Test complete user workflows from start to finish
- Use real browser automation tools
- Test critical user paths and edge cases
- Verify that the entire system works as expected

### Performance Testing

- Test performance-critical code with timing assertions
- Use performance benchmarks for optimization validation
- Test memory usage and garbage collection
- Verify that performance requirements are met

```typescript
describe('Performance Tests', () => {
  it('should process large dataset within time limit', () => {
    const startTime = performance.now();

    // Act
    const result = processLargeDataset(largeData);

    const endTime = performance.now();
    const duration = endTime - startTime;

    // Assert
    expect(duration).to.be.lessThan(1000); // 1 second limit
    expect(result).to.have.length(largeData.length);
  });
});
```

### Error Testing

- Test error conditions and edge cases
- Verify that appropriate error messages are returned
- Test error handling and recovery mechanisms
- Ensure that errors are logged appropriately

```typescript
describe('Error Handling', () => {
  it('should throw error for invalid input', () => {
    expect(() => {
      processData(null);
    }).to.throw('Input cannot be null');
  });

  it('should handle network errors gracefully', async () => {
    nock('https://api.example.com').get('/data').replyWithError('Network error');

    const result = await apiClient.getData();
    expect(result).to.be.null;
  });
});
```

## Examples

```typescript
// Complete TDD example
describe('StringCalculator', () => {
  describe('add', () => {
    it('should return 0 for empty string', () => {
      const calculator = new StringCalculator();
      const result = calculator.add('');
      expect(result).to.equal(0);
    });

    it('should return sum for single number', () => {
      const calculator = new StringCalculator();
      const result = calculator.add('5');
      expect(result).to.equal(5);
    });

    it('should return sum for two numbers', () => {
      const calculator = new StringCalculator();
      const result = calculator.add('1,2');
      expect(result).to.equal(3);
    });
  });
});
```
